{"question": "Loss of flexion in the interphalangeal joint of thumb in supracondylar fracture is due to involvement of which nerve?", "options": {"A": "Anterior interosseous", "B": "Posterior interosseous", "C": "Median", "D": "Ulnar"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Orthopedics", "Neurology", "Hand Surgery", "Traumatology", "Anatomy"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Orthopedics"], "question_analyses": {"Orthopedics": "The scenario described involves a patient with a supracondylar fracture of the humerus, which is a fracture that occurs just above the elbow joint. The specific symptom mentioned is the loss of flexion in the interphalangeal joint of the thumb. This symptom is indicative of a nerve injury, specifically involving the recurrent branch of the median nerve. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n- **Supracondylar Fracture**: This type of fracture is common in children and can also occur in adults, often due to a fall onto an outstretched hand. It involves the distal humerus, which is the lower end of the upper arm bone. - **Loss of Flexion in the Interphalangeal Joint of the Thumb**: The interphalangeal joint of the thumb is responsible for the flexion and extension movements of the thumb. The loss of flexion suggests that the muscle responsible for flexing the thumb (the flexor pollicis brevis and the flexor pollicis longus) is not functioning properly. - **Involvement of the Recurrent Branch of the Median Nerve**: The median nerve, which originates from the brachial plexus, has a branch that loops around the flexor retinaculum and supplies the thenar muscles, including the flexor pollicis brevis. This branch is particularly vulnerable in supracondylar fractures due to its anatomical position..", "Neurology": "The scenario described involves a patient with a supracondylar fracture of the humerus, which is a fracture that occurs just above the elbow joint. The specific symptom mentioned is the loss of flexion in the interphalangeal joint of the thumb. This symptom is indicative of a nerve injury, specifically involving the recurrent branch of the median nerve.\n\n### Interpretation of the Condition:\n- **Supracondylar Fracture**: This type of fracture is common in children and can also occur in adults, often due to a fall onto an outstretched hand. It involves the distal humerus, which is the lower end of the upper arm bone.\n- **Loss of Flexion in the Interphalangeal Joint of the Thumb**: This symptom suggests that the patient is unable to bend the tip of their thumb, which is a function controlled by the recurrent branch of the median nerve. The median nerve runs through the arm and forearm and provides motor and sensory innervation to parts of the hand, including the thenar muscles (which control the movement of the thumb).\n\n### Noteworthy Aspects:\n1. **Nerve Involvement**: The most alarming aspect of this scenario is the involvement of the recurrent branch of the median nerve. This nerve is crucial for the function of the thenar muscles, which are responsible for the movement of the thumb. Injury to this branch can lead to significant functional impairment of the hand, particularly affecting the ability to grasp and manipulate objects.\n", "Hand Surgery": "The scenario described involves a patient with a supracondylar fracture of the humerus, which is a fracture located above the elbow joint. The specific symptom mentioned is the loss of flexion in the interphalangeal joint of the thumb. This symptom is indicative of a nerve injury, specifically involving the recurrent branch of the median nerve. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n- **Supracondylar Fracture**: This type of fracture is common in children and can also occur in adults, often due to a fall onto an outstretched hand. It involves the distal humerus, which is the lower end of the upper arm bone. - **Loss of Flexion in the Interphalangeal Joint of the Thumb**: The interphalangeal joint of the thumb is responsible fo